Improvements made to the initial SRS 
One of the improvements made to generated SRS was to keep the functional, non-functional requirements and additional functionalities atomic, clear and precise. The original SRS generated by the LLM covers all the functional, non-functional requirements and additional functionalities but each requirement is too complex and detailed, which would affect how the requirements are translated to the flowchart and pseudocode later. Keeping it atomic and simpler would make implementation a lot easier to follow and saves time. 
Additionally, I had double-checked the generated srs to ensure that all requirements were met and ensured that the requirements followed a logical sequential order, which ensured that it makes it easier to follow step by step before planning the flowchart and pseudocode to reduce the chance making mistakes beforehand. 



IMPROVED SOFTWARE REQUIREMENTS SPECIFICATION 
functional requirements
1)	The noughts and crosses game will need a physical board for the swiftbot to move
2)	An ascii gameboard will be displayed in CLI console with 9 squares and 4 game pieces for each player: X and O, to mark their chosen position.
3)	The physical board will be a 3x3 with 9 squares and size of 75x75 cm and will be numbered [1-3]. Each square will measure 25x25 cm.
4)	the program will need to align each of the 4 swiftbot buttons with a chosen colour and should display the corresponding colour when the button is pressed: 
a)	Button A (front left): Red 
b)	Button B (back left): Green
c)	Button X (front right): Blue
d)	Button Y (back right): Yellow
5)	The program will need to display a welcome screen with a user-readable message. It should have a brief description of the game rules, which must also be clear.
6)	The program shall begin the registration sequence when the user presses the SwiftBot ‘A’ button.
7)	the program should prompt the user to enter a username via the keyboard. The name should be in a string format; any other formats is invalid. 
8)	a name will be randomly generated and assigned to the swiftbot which can be configured by the user by option. 
9)	The System shall record the board specification as 3×3 squares, each 25 cm × 25 cm, overall board 75 cm × 75 cm, and use cell coordinates in the range (row=1-3, col=1-3).
10)	The program will roll a virtual 6-sided die for both the player and swiftbot automatically. 
11)	The player with the highest dice roll goes first and will be assigned the ‘O’ piece, whilst the other will have the ‘X’ piece. if there’s a tie, the system shall re-roll until a single higher value is produced and the starter is determined. 
12)	This will be presented in an ASCII 3x3 board in the console, with clear row/column labels before the first move and after every move. 
13)	When it is the User’s turn, the System shall prompt the User to enter a move in the format [row, column] with integers 1-3. 
14)	The System shall validate that user input is numeric, follows [row, column] syntax, and that row and column values are each within 1-3. If invalid, the System shall show an error and re-prompt. 
15)	The System shall check whether the selected square is empty; if occupied, the System shall notify the User and re-prompt for a different move. 
16)	When the User provides a valid move, the System shall mark the internal board state with the User’s piece (‘O’ or ‘X’) and update the displayed ASCII board.
17)	After each turn, the program will display the username, playing piece and position as a visual presentation, such as the format ‘[Yasoda – O] moved to square [r, c] [SwiftBot – X] moved to square [r, c])’. 

18)	When it’s the swiftbots turn, the robot will randomly select its move to its target square using the numerical range and display the chosen square to the User before instructing the SwiftBot to move.
19)	After going to the chosen square, it will then blink in green lights 3 times after that and then return to its starting position in the board, and the system should display the following summary:

[PlayerName – Piece] moved to square [r, c]

20)	The System shall enforce alternating turns between players and prevent any player from making two consecutive moves.
21)	The program will determine if the game has ended in a win or draw via tracing the winning line and selectively blinking its lights.
22)	If the user wins, the swiftbot will trace the winning line and blinks green 3 times before and after tracing for an ‘O’ win. 
23)	If the swiftbot wins, the swiftbot will trace the winning line and blinks red 3 times before and after tracing for an ‘X’ win.
24)	If it’s a draw, the swiftbot will spin once and blink in blue 3 times before and after the move. 
25)	After each round ends (win or draw), the System shall append a persistent log record containing round number, full move sequence (player name, piece, [row, col] in order), result (win/draw), winner name if any, and timestamp in ISO format.
26)	The System shall maintain a scoreboard across rounds, increment the winner’s score when a round is won, and display the scoreboard after each round.
27)	After a round, the System shall prompt the User to select the following:

Button Y: play around round which the system will reset the board and start a new round
Button X: quit and terminate the program 

28)	If the User chooses to quit, the System shall flush and close the log file, display the absolute file path of the log file, and terminate the program cleanly.
29)	The System shall maintain the SwiftBot’s current board cell, orientation, and calibration parameters (seconds-per-25cm) in persistent/volatile state and use them for movement planning; the System shall detect calibration errors and refuse motion if calibration is out of acceptable bounds.
30)	The swiftbot will keep track of its current location and orientation within the game board where it depends on the current direction its facing and how it should move to the next square. 
31)	All the game inputs and outputs will be handled via the command line interface console. 
Non-functional requirements 
1)	The game interface will be implemented as a simple command line interface console 
2)	There will be effective and reliable input validation to check the format, data type and numeric ranges for user input. 
3)	Error message should be clear, concise and on to the point. Non-technical users should not see that confusing paragraph/block of error message. 
4)	Efficient error-handling to manage exceptions, prevent unexpected outcomes like crashes and bugs and handle it gracefully. 
5)	Console layout design formatting and structure should be clear, simple and organised, displaying clear information.
6)	Displays game board in console game in clear and precise format. 
7)	The response time should be shorter from 1-2 seconds for updating player moves, calculating positions, updating boards. 
8)	Calculates the correct player piece positions, accurate calculated scores, correct detection of win/lose/draw states of each player.
9)	A log file that consistently tracks and records game events and actions like player names, scoring, player positions, movements, wins, losses and draws. 
10)	Program code structure should be modular, organised so it is readable. 
11)	Code comments should be provided to explain each method on its function and purpose. 
12)	Swiftbot distance, speed and time to travel should be controlled to match acceptable ranges before and during movements across the squares. 
13)	Overall, the program should prioritize usability and user experience considerations. 
14)	The game logic should be tested independently without connection to the swift Bot itself. 



Additional functionalities 
1)	Choice to undo the last move the player makes but has a limit of 2 times (including the players first move) to prevent them from too easily gaining an advantage and allow them to think quicker and more strategically to balance out the odds of the game. 
2)	a timer will be set where the user will have to put their piece on the game board and will allow the player to adjust from 5-10 seconds the time the program will recognise the validity of the move. After that time, the move will become invalid and will give that turn to the next player. 
3)	The game will have a difficulty settings option at the start of the game, in the introduction, where the command line interface will display options, which are ‘easy’, ‘medium’ and ‘hard’ after the introduction of the game, asking the user ‘what game mode? Enter easy, medium or hard’. the difficulty of the game will depend on the two functionalities stated above: 
a)	How much ‘undo’s’ the user will have depending on game difficulty. For instance, the ‘easy’ mode will have 2 new move undo’s, meaning the user can revise their move choices back by removing their piece twice. Whereas the medium and hard modes will have 1 and none respectively. 
b)	This will prompt the program to ask, ‘do you want to undo your last move, enter yes or no?’, if yes, the gameboard will remove your last piece to start again or if no, then the pieces stays there. 
c)	The program will keep track of the number of times the user uses the undo option based on their difficulty. This value will be enclosed in brackets like ‘Undo move chances: 2’ after the program asks yes or no. this will be shown in the command line as ‘do you want to undo your last move, enter yes or no? (Undo move chances: 2)’, 
d)	For each turn, a timer that will dictate how long the player is allowed to think before putting down their piece. Failure to decide which square to place the piece within the timeframe will make the turn invalid by the program. For the easy mode, there won’t be a timer but a time limit of 10 seconds for the medium mode and 5 seconds for the hard mode will be applied. 
